WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.340
A brief made by PGR,
placed in the agent's prompt.

2
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.061
70k to 71k tokens, in 1 turn.

3
00:00:11.661 --> 00:00:14.041
Fully correct in 12 of 12 runs.

4
00:00:15.641 --> 00:00:22.046
72% to 80% fewer tokens than
no index, across 4 comparisons.

5
00:00:25.123 --> 00:00:30.252
PeterGanuRusty is a desktop
command-line tool for AI coding agents.

6
00:00:31.852 --> 00:00:35.195
Written in Rust. It runs
locally, with no account.

7
00:00:37.661 --> 00:00:39.556
Find a function and its callers.

8
00:00:41.156 --> 00:00:43.484
Find code by describing the job.

9
00:00:45.084 --> 00:00:46.635
See what a change reaches.

10
00:00:48.235 --> 00:00:50.146
Keep findings between sessions.

11
00:00:52.738 --> 00:00:55.171
Here is the measured token comparison.

12
00:00:56.771 --> 00:01:02.372
294k without an index. 71k with the brief.

13
00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:08.888
76% fewer tokens.
Claude Haiku 5.5, low effort,

14
00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:10.936
first question set.

15
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:16.513
One repository. A few runs per cell.

16
00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:21.425
The builders wrote the questions,
including the second set.

17
00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:27.140
These were code lookups.
Open-ended coding work was not tested.

18
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.713
The study tests finding definitions
and code by description.

19
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.818
PeterGanuRusty. One command installs it.
